WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.805
The anatomy of an LLM. What you are actually buying, in under three minutes.

2
00:00:07.205 --> 00:00:10.536
When you send a message, the model never sees just your question.

3
00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:17.983
The system prompt, the whole conversation, retrieved documents and tool outputs are stitched into one long context.

4
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:19.443
And all of it is billed.

5
00:00:22.443 --> 00:00:28.267
A tokenizer chops that text into sub-word pieces called tokens, and each one becomes a number.

6
00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:30.966
"The cat sat on the" is five tokens.

7
00:00:30.966 --> 00:00:33.215
Tokens are the unit on your invoice.

8
00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:39.094
Each number then looks up a vector of thousands of values.

9
00:00:39.094 --> 00:00:40.933
Meaning, encoded as geometry.

10
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:47.766
Those vectors flow through a stack of about eighty identical transformer layers.

11
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:53.590
Inside every layer, attention lets each token look back at the ones before it.

12
00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.528
The last "the" pulls hardest on "sat" and "cat".

13
00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:04.790
At the top of the stack, the model scores every one of a hundred thousand possible next tokens.

14
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.950
"Mat" wins, and a sampler picks it.

15
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.004
Then that token is fed straight back in, and the whole stack runs again.

16
00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:15.799
One full pass, for every word you see streaming onto your screen.

17
00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:21.025
That loop splits every request into two phases.

18
00:01:21.474 --> 00:01:25.954
Prefill reads your entire prompt in one parallel pass. Fast, and cheap.

19
00:01:26.404 --> 00:01:32.683
Decode writes the answer one token at a time, and every token re-reads all of the model's weights from memory.

20
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:37.059
That is why output tokens cost three to eight times more than input.

21
00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:41.011
The same physics makes the discounts honest.

22
00:01:41.011 --> 00:01:44.757
Cached prompts skip re-reading, for about ninety percent off.

23
00:01:44.757 --> 00:01:46.599
Batch lanes run at half price.

24
00:01:48.999 --> 00:01:52.239
Finally, five hidden dials quietly change your bill.

25
00:01:52.239 --> 00:01:57.347
Two are set by the model maker, two by whoever serves the model, and one is yours.

26
00:01:58.197 --> 00:01:59.464
One. The tokenizer.

27
00:01:59.464 --> 00:02:04.798
The same sentence costs almost seven times more tokens in Hindi than in English.

28
00:02:06.247 --> 00:02:07.376
Two. Architecture.

29
00:02:07.376 --> 00:02:16.655
A trillion-parameter mixture of experts wakes up only about thirty-seven billion per token, so it can be cheaper than a dense seventy-billion model.

30
00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:19.124
Three. Precision.

31
00:02:19.124 --> 00:02:24.339
Going from sixteen bits to eight, to four, halves the memory read per token, each step.

32
00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:26.931
Four. Batching.

33
00:02:26.931 --> 00:02:33.025
Thirty-two requests share one read of the weights, cutting cost per token by over ninety percent.

34
00:02:33.025 --> 00:02:34.596
The biggest lever of all.

35
00:02:36.046 --> 00:02:38.036
And five. The context you send.

36
00:02:38.036 --> 00:02:42.337
Your system prompt is re-sent, and re-billed, on every single turn.

37
00:02:44.937 --> 00:02:47.772
Sequential generation. Memory. Weight reads.

38
00:02:47.772 --> 00:02:51.186
That is the machine behind every price in this guide.

39
00:02:52.436 --> 00:02:55.440
Now open each chapter, and try the dials yourself.
